{"text": " You can know 400 words and still freeze ordering a coffee. That's what happened to Anna and the fix wasn't more words, it was one list she wrote in 10 minutes. Anna was moving to Madrid in 12 weeks, so every night she learned 20 new words in an app. Six weeks in she knew 400 words, then she walked into a cafe and couldn't say one full sentence. The words weren't wrong, she just never said them out loud together. So what did she change? She wrote down the 20 sentences she actually says in a normal day. A coffee please, can you say that slower, where's the metro? She translated them once, then every morning she said all 20 out loud and recorded one minute, about five minutes a day. Week 10 Anna ordered, asked for the bill and chatted with her landlord, in Spanish. Here's the rule, write the 20 sentences you say every day, say them out loud each morning, record one minute, send this to the friend with a long order of coffee. Anna's plan started with one list, yours can too, that's what demure is for, you archive your dream today so you can see it tomorrow and break it down into small steps you can actually do. Follow for one fix a day, tonight, why you forget most of a book within a week. If you want to try it out, I've put the link in the bio.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 5.0, "text": " You can know 400 words and still freeze ordering a coffee. That's what happened to Anna and the", "tokens": [50363, 921, 460, 760, 7337, 2456, 290, 991, 16611, 16216, 257, 6891, 13, 1320, 338, 644, 3022, 284, 11735, 290, 262, 50607], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.07788010132618439, "compression_ratio": 1.6523297491039426, "no_speech_prob": 0.00047951765009202063, "words": [{"word": " You", "start": 0.0, "end": 0.24, "probability": 0.594737708568573}, {"word": " can", "start": 0.24, "end": 0.38, "probability": 0.9941775798797607}, {"word": " know", "start": 0.38, "end": 0.58, "probability": 0.986639678478241}, {"word": " 400", "start": 0.58